01:09:06,928 --> 01:09:12,830
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, I think Rita just read this is the most, the largest KOA campground in the country.

1400
01:09:12,850 --> 01:09:13,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that what you read?

1401
01:09:13,611 --> 01:09:14,091
No.

1402
01:09:14,711 --> 01:09:14,951
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1403
01:09:16,212 --> 01:09:16,832
[SPEAKER_02]: What did you read?

1404
01:09:17,072 --> 01:09:18,893
[SPEAKER_02]: I read that it's so tense.

1405
01:09:20,993 --> 01:09:22,314
[SPEAKER_01]: No, she read something else.

1406
01:09:22,374 --> 01:09:25,876
[SPEAKER_01]: She read the state capitol building, which we saw this morning, was the largest in the country.

1407
01:09:25,936 --> 01:09:26,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that what you read?

1408
01:09:29,358 --> 01:09:31,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, anyhow, something was the largest in the country, whatever it was.

1409
01:09:32,380 --> 01:09:35,382
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was a pretty big campground, even though it may not have been the biggest.

1410
01:09:35,622 --> 01:09:39,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And we had a tiny corner there right next to the woodshed.

1411
01:09:41,426 --> 01:09:44,948
[SPEAKER_01]: A1, it was called.

1412
01:09:46,108 --> 01:09:48,630
[SPEAKER_01]: I called him from the office on Friday night.

1413
01:09:50,129 --> 01:09:53,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And right away, he said he'd gotten my card, my letter.

1414
01:09:54,810 --> 01:09:59,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And we began talking about where we could meet.

1415
01:09:59,592 --> 01:10:02,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was tied up Saturday morning.

1416
01:10:03,173 --> 01:10:04,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And he had some time Saturday afternoon.

1417
01:10:05,133 --> 01:10:08,914
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't have any room at his place, nothing there.

1418
01:10:10,495 --> 01:10:12,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And so he was going to come out to my place.

1419
01:10:13,715 --> 01:10:14,836
[SPEAKER_01]: And he wondered where I was.

1420
01:10:14,876 --> 01:10:15,856
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I'm living in a tent.

1421
01:10:15,876 --> 01:10:17,056
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, what, a tent?

1422
01:10:18,177 --> 01:10:19,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And I thought the interview was dead then.

1423
01:10:21,805 --> 01:10:24,306
[SPEAKER_01]: He must have thought I was some hillbilly from West Virginia.

1424
01:10:26,307 --> 01:10:30,349
[SPEAKER_01]: But I said, yeah, we're camping our way across the country.

1425
01:10:30,429 --> 01:10:33,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think we have electric power at our campsite.

1426
01:10:33,731 --> 01:10:49,019
[SPEAKER_01]: And if we had no place at his home, then if he could come out here, why, yes, that would be fine on a lovely June afternoon with the sun beaming down, nice and comfortable under the shaded trees there.

1427
01:10:50,549 --> 01:10:53,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I gave him the wrong place to cut off.

1428
01:10:53,191 --> 01:10:57,355
[SPEAKER_01]: I said Cannon Street, when it was Congress Street.

1429
01:10:58,596 --> 01:11:00,137
[SPEAKER_01]: As it turned out, that didn't matter very much.

1430
01:11:03,420 --> 01:11:04,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So he agreed.

1431
01:11:04,541 --> 01:11:06,703
[SPEAKER_01]: 2 o'clock, he'd come out to our campsite.

1432
01:11:08,685 --> 01:11:11,968
[SPEAKER_01]: And we'd have the interview there at our table, maybe.

1433
01:11:13,429 --> 01:11:15,671
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd say, give me an hour, so then you'd have to leave.

1434
01:11:17,873 --> 01:11:29,401
[SPEAKER_01]: All right, so I was quite pleased he was willing to talk to me, although the idea he wouldn't let me come to his house, I was somewhat suspect about that.

1435
01:11:31,843 --> 01:11:35,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Friday night started out very nicely.

1436
01:11:35,586 --> 01:11:40,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Then there began to be heard the little pitter patter of raindrops on our roof.

1437
01:11:42,470 --> 01:11:46,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And maybe over on Rita's side, a little bit of drip, drip, drip inside the roof.

1438
01:11:48,298 --> 01:11:50,999
[SPEAKER_01]: We hadn't waterproofed our roof too well, as it turned out.

1439
01:11:51,479 --> 01:11:54,200
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was a rather unusual rain all night long.

1440
01:11:54,280 --> 01:11:57,941
[SPEAKER_01]: It just dripped and dropped.

1441
01:11:58,001 --> 01:12:04,944
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't a heavy downpour, although when you got up in the morning and looked out at some of the puddles laying around, it seemed like it must have been a lot of water.

1442
01:12:05,684 --> 01:12:08,645
[SPEAKER_01]: And maybe it was, but it came rather slowly but steadily.

1443
01:12:10,065 --> 01:12:14,087
[SPEAKER_01]: And we were pretty dry in the morning, except Rita had her, what was wet?

1444
01:12:15,650 --> 01:12:16,231
[SPEAKER_01]: her blanket.

1445
01:12:16,591 --> 01:12:29,382
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought it was something else because her blanket was a little bit wet where the little bit of water had gathered into a kind of a gulf on the tent where it sagged a little bit and it began to drip through since it couldn't drain off.

1446
01:12:31,704 --> 01:12:40,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we got up, had our breakfast with eggs and sausage and it was a good breakfast and the rain was pretty much

1447
01:12:41,137 --> 01:12:47,782
[SPEAKER_01]: stopped by then, but the clouds were somewhat threatening and it was kind of an on and off proposition throughout the morning.

1448
01:12:49,343 --> 01:12:51,164
[SPEAKER_01]: I spent the morning writing in my journal.

1449
01:12:52,765 --> 01:12:54,426
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know what she spent the morning doing.

1450
01:12:56,047 --> 01:12:57,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, she reorganized.

1451
01:12:57,888 --> 01:12:58,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1452
01:12:58,589 --> 01:13:02,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Did a lot of things which had to be done and had gathered up over the past few days.

1453
01:13:05,454 --> 01:13:07,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Then along towards noon,

1454
01:13:08,915 --> 01:13:11,896
[SPEAKER_01]: It began to rain again, and we began to put all of our stuff inside.

1455
01:13:13,816 --> 01:13:16,517
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't a heavy rain, but it was a steady little drizzle.

1456
01:13:17,857 --> 01:13:23,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So I hustled off to the headquarters and phoned Bibb Falk.

1457
01:13:24,379 --> 01:13:26,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, it might be kind of muddy out here.

1458
01:13:26,819 --> 01:13:29,060
[SPEAKER_01]: And besides, I gave you the wrong direction to get out here.

1459
01:13:29,100 --> 01:13:30,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said it was Congress, not Cannon.

1460
01:13:32,381 --> 01:13:34,141
[SPEAKER_01]: And we discussed that a little bit.

1461
01:13:34,662 --> 01:13:39,403
[SPEAKER_01]: But by this time, he was much more receptive to my coming to his house.

1462
01:13:41,323 --> 01:13:47,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Rita suggested maybe he call Ted Lyons, who guaranteed my credentials.

1463
01:13:47,104 --> 01:13:53,625
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know, but he was much more receptive to my coming to his house this time.

1464
01:13:54,005 --> 01:13:56,286
[SPEAKER_01]: And so that was decided upon.

1465
01:13:58,126 --> 01:14:01,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And the rain began to get a little bit heavier.

1466
01:14:01,627 --> 01:14:03,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Right away was the interview, because

1467
01:14:06,168 --> 01:14:07,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Apparently it had something to do with it.

1468
01:14:07,348 --> 01:14:09,089
[SPEAKER_01]: I later found out he wanted to watch the ball game.

1469
01:14:11,250 --> 01:14:23,576
[SPEAKER_01]: So I rushed back to the tent and gathered all my belongings in my little case, the tapes, the recorder, the extension cords, the autographed baseball, the encyclopedia.

1470
01:14:23,616 --> 01:14:24,997
[SPEAKER_01]: And off I went.

1471
01:14:25,037 --> 01:14:31,820
[SPEAKER_01]: And the rain became heavier as I proceeded northward on Interstate Highway 35 into the heart of downtown

1472
01:14:32,637 --> 01:14:34,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Austin, although we bypassed it.

1473
01:14:34,817 --> 01:14:35,498
[SPEAKER_01]: We went over it.

1474
01:14:35,578 --> 01:14:38,978
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw the Holiday Inn on the way in.

1475
01:14:39,058 --> 01:14:43,459
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw the Texas football stadium on one side.

1476
01:14:44,799 --> 01:14:50,620
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw a big round building, which turned out to be the Texas University Coliseum.

1477
01:14:51,540 --> 01:14:53,821
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't see the Texas University baseball field.

1478
01:14:53,861 --> 01:14:58,802
[SPEAKER_01]: It was on the other side, but at a point where the road was rather low, and I couldn't see up to it.

1479
01:14:59,322 --> 01:15:00,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Big Falk told me about that later.

1480
01:15:02,112 --> 01:15:09,958
[SPEAKER_01]: As you see in the tape, it was a $2.5 million college baseball plant, which has to take the cake throughout the country.

1481
01:15:10,698 --> 01:15:21,687
[SPEAKER_01]: We had a chance to see it this morning with its tartan turf and its 5,000 seat grandstand, the lights, the beautiful layout there.

1482
01:15:21,727 --> 01:15:23,028
[SPEAKER_01]: I took a few pictures of it.

1483
01:15:25,565 --> 01:15:53,806
[SPEAKER_01]: credit to any triple a minor league ball cause there's no question about better than any I've seen maybe not quite as good as The Franklin County Stadium, but better than any other Now so I raced up route 35 to 38 and a half Street is where I cut off and worked my way over to D Avenue or to Speedway Avenue then up to 42nd over to D and up D to Bib Fox house where he

1484
01:15:54,830 --> 01:15:59,554
[SPEAKER_01]: who is a bachelor, and his maiden sister, 86 years old.

1485
01:15:59,574 --> 01:16:00,655
[SPEAKER_01]: He's only 79.

1486
01:16:01,135 --> 01:16:09,561
[SPEAKER_01]: They lived together there in this little wooden frame house, not very elaborate at all, at the corner of D and 43rd Street.

1487
01:16:11,122 --> 01:16:14,605
[SPEAKER_01]: He was sitting out on the porch as I rolled in.

1488
01:16:15,005 --> 01:16:21,190
[SPEAKER_01]: I hadn't eaten any lunch, so quickly after the phone call, I had bought two large Hershey bars.

1489
01:16:21,842 --> 01:16:27,483
[SPEAKER_01]: And I ate those Hershey bars on the way up, jamming them down and not digesting them too well.

1490
01:16:27,523 --> 01:16:28,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But that was my lunch.

1491
01:16:29,284 --> 01:16:33,344
[SPEAKER_01]: And I got my hands into some sticky, the Hershey bars got a little sticky there.

1492
01:16:33,424 --> 01:16:34,565
[SPEAKER_01]: My hands are kind of sticky.

1493
01:16:35,485 --> 01:16:37,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And I tried to look around in the car.

1494
01:16:37,345 --> 01:16:43,986
[SPEAKER_01]: I stopped just before I got to the corner of the street there to look around for a wash and wipe to wipe off the stickiness.

1495
01:16:44,207 --> 01:16:48,207
[SPEAKER_01]: But as you might suspect, there was no wash and wipe to be seen anywhere in that car.

1496
01:16:50,708 --> 01:16:51,548
[SPEAKER_01]: They were in her gritchel.

1497
01:16:53,162 --> 01:16:57,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So I went in this interview with sticky hands.

1498
01:16:57,783 --> 01:17:00,664
[SPEAKER_01]: But I rolled down slowly and pulled up in front of this house.

1499
01:17:00,724 --> 01:17:04,385
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was Bibb Falk out on the front porch waiting for me, walking out.

1500
01:17:04,745 --> 01:17:06,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And I recognized him instantly.

1501
01:17:06,966 --> 01:17:10,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Big, tall, handsome guy, slim.

1502
01:17:11,527 --> 01:17:19,150
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody who's almost 80 years old, how they can retain the almost identical look of youth, it's hard to imagine.

1503
01:17:19,190 --> 01:17:20,670
[SPEAKER_01]: We saw Ted Lyons over in Vinton.

1504
01:17:21,690 --> 01:17:24,371
[SPEAKER_01]: And with his glasses on, he looked pretty much like the Ted lines of youth.

1505
01:17:24,391 --> 01:17:25,532
[SPEAKER_01]: When he took them off, he didn't.

1506
01:17:26,852 --> 01:17:30,754
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was about four or five years younger than Bibb Falk, or three years younger.

1507
01:17:31,655 --> 01:17:33,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Falk didn't have those lines around his eyes.

1508
01:17:33,675 --> 01:17:44,080
[SPEAKER_01]: He had that sturdy, upright gait, that clear complexion, all of his hair neatly combed back.

1509
01:17:44,400 --> 01:17:45,821
[SPEAKER_01]: A very handsome man.

1510
01:17:47,142 --> 01:17:50,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So he came out and greeted me in the street there and wondered where my gear was.

1511
01:17:50,885 --> 01:17:51,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And I got it out.

1512
01:17:51,765 --> 01:17:53,627
[SPEAKER_01]: And he held the door as I got it out.

1513
01:17:53,647 --> 01:17:54,627
[SPEAKER_01]: And we went into the house.

1514
01:17:56,048 --> 01:17:58,150
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't, as I say, a very impressive house.

1515
01:17:59,771 --> 01:18:01,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was a house.

1516
01:18:02,293 --> 01:18:02,933
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was neat.

1517
01:18:04,014 --> 01:18:05,695
[SPEAKER_01]: And we sat up in the living room there.

1518
01:18:06,536 --> 01:18:09,918
[SPEAKER_01]: And there weren't many elaborate furnishings.

1519
01:18:09,938 --> 01:18:16,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And I didn't see his sister at all, except one time late in the interview, she came into the dining room, which was the room behind the living room there.

1520
01:18:16,923 --> 01:18:19,045
[SPEAKER_01]: to get something when retired back to the kitchen.

1521
01:18:20,866 --> 01:18:32,375
[SPEAKER_01]: We sat in adjacent chairs with the recorder in between us, and away we went on this interview.

1522
01:18:32,455 --> 01:18:34,036
[SPEAKER_01]: It was not an easy interview.

1523
01:18:35,577 --> 01:18:38,559
[SPEAKER_01]: He was somewhat restrained.

1524
01:18:39,520 --> 01:18:42,282
[SPEAKER_01]: He did warm up a little bit, and he was friendly.

1525
01:18:42,662 --> 01:18:44,664
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't have too much to say about some things.

1526
01:18:46,728 --> 01:18:49,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Some things he wouldn't talk about.

1527
01:18:50,050 --> 01:18:53,331
[SPEAKER_01]: The black socks business, he seemed to talk about as much as he knew.

1528
01:18:54,532 --> 01:18:56,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But he didn't volunteer a lot of things.

1529
01:18:56,393 --> 01:19:01,815
[SPEAKER_01]: I had to kind of keep probing and prying, and sometimes to no avail.

1530
01:19:02,976 --> 01:19:15,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Once in a while, I hit something which he would respond to, like Johnny Mossel, Alex Metzler, Charlie Robertson, the no-hit game there, which he did not play, but was on the bench.

1531
01:19:18,230 --> 01:19:19,411
[SPEAKER_01]: A little bit on Babe Ruth.

1532
01:19:19,671 --> 01:19:37,778
[SPEAKER_01]: But he just, from all I've heard about Bib Fock, the jockey Fock, this kind of a hail fellow well-meant, I thought he would be an outgoing, talkative, really very energetic, enthusiastic person in an easy interview.

1533
01:19:38,318 --> 01:19:39,418
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was not that way.

1534
01:19:39,438 --> 01:19:41,939
[SPEAKER_01]: I had trouble filling up an hour of tape.

1535
01:19:42,860 --> 01:19:46,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And a good bit of the tape was on his college coaching days.

1536
01:19:48,043 --> 01:19:53,786
[SPEAKER_01]: which really was of interest generally, but not of my primary concern.

1537
01:19:56,687 --> 01:19:59,228
[SPEAKER_01]: I happened to mention the Hall of Fame once or twice.

1538
01:19:59,248 --> 01:20:11,553
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't want to talk about that, whether he's bitter that he isn't in the Hall of Fame, or he thinks some of the people in there are not worthy of it.

1539
01:20:12,183 --> 01:20:12,643
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

1540
01:20:12,723 --> 01:20:15,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And a couple of things I mentioned, he just seemed to want to close the book on it.

1541
01:20:15,924 --> 01:20:16,944
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't want to talk about them.

1542
01:20:16,964 --> 01:20:18,844
[SPEAKER_01]: He was quite curt in saying this.

1543
01:20:20,084 --> 01:20:25,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So I was a little ill at ease at times in this interview.

1544
01:20:25,345 --> 01:20:32,006
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't quite, while my credentials were rather quickly established, I couldn't warm up to him.

1545
01:20:32,026 --> 01:20:36,067
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't really exchange ideas and thoughts the way I have in so many of these interviews.

1546
01:20:36,087 --> 01:20:41,928
[SPEAKER_01]: There was a certain stiffness which prevailed in this, in the course of the interview.

1547
01:20:43,132 --> 01:20:44,432
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't completely at ease.

1548
01:20:47,373 --> 01:20:51,814
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he was probably as friendly as he ever is with anybody who walks in off the street like this.

1549
01:20:52,854 --> 01:20:57,916
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm convinced I probably got as much as anyone did from an interview with Bibb Falk.

1550
01:20:57,936 --> 01:21:03,597
[SPEAKER_01]: But I just had expected, from what I'd heard, so much more than what I actually got.

1551
01:21:04,957 --> 01:21:05,798
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you doing now?

1552
01:21:07,878 --> 01:21:08,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Huh?

1553
01:21:09,479 --> 01:21:11,139
[SPEAKER_01]: She's spraying the interior of the tent.

1554
01:21:12,609 --> 01:21:17,871
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the first interview I've ever commented on in the wide open spaces, I might say.

1555
01:21:19,271 --> 01:21:20,812
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's such a beautiful night.

1556
01:21:21,112 --> 01:21:22,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all dark now.

1557
01:21:22,132 --> 01:21:26,434
[SPEAKER_01]: The lights in the camp surrounding us.

1558
01:21:27,754 --> 01:21:30,395
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's just a glorious night.

1559
01:21:31,196 --> 01:21:33,216
[SPEAKER_01]: You can hear the crickets in the distance, probably.

1560
01:21:35,577 --> 01:21:38,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we kind of dragged this thing out a couple of times in the interview.

1561
01:21:39,019 --> 01:21:39,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Just almost died.

1562
01:21:40,997 --> 01:21:43,418
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think I prepared well enough for this.

1563
01:21:43,538 --> 01:21:56,800
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure if I had spent about a half hour going over his record and picking out incidents in his career with the White Sox and with Cleveland, I could have done a better job.

1564
01:21:56,820 --> 01:21:59,401
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was spontaneous there.

1565
01:22:00,921 --> 01:22:04,602
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I'd planned to spend a little time preparing prior to this coming out at 2 o'clock.

1566
01:22:04,662 --> 01:22:08,043
[SPEAKER_01]: But then this thing came up so quickly, my going in there, I didn't have time.

1567
01:22:11,391 --> 01:22:15,672
[SPEAKER_01]: I had a few general questions, but I didn't have enough of a specific character.

1568
01:22:17,092 --> 01:22:26,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And that could have, I'm sure, enriched the interview had I been a little better prepared for it.

1569
01:22:26,874 --> 01:22:34,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, after about an hour or so and some hemming and hawing, I said, well, I'd like to take this picture just here at the end of the tape there.

1570
01:22:35,255 --> 01:22:36,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So I shut the machine off, and I

1571
01:22:39,990 --> 01:22:42,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I asked to see his sister, too.

1572
01:22:42,431 --> 01:22:43,392
[SPEAKER_01]: And he brought her in.

1573
01:22:43,432 --> 01:22:43,912
[SPEAKER_01]: I met her.

1574
01:22:44,012 --> 01:22:53,616
[SPEAKER_01]: And she invited me back to the kitchen, where he's got a few pictures up on the wall, but mostly his Texas University coaching career.

1575
01:22:54,856 --> 01:23:00,519
[SPEAKER_01]: There was one nice picture of him in a white sex uniform, about a 5 by 7 head shot.

1576
01:23:01,759 --> 01:23:05,321
[SPEAKER_01]: There was one good one of his trip to England in 1934, I think it was, or 1924.

1577
01:23:09,212 --> 01:23:13,154
[SPEAKER_01]: when he went with the White Sox and the Giants to England.

1578
01:23:13,234 --> 01:23:34,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And it shows all of the White Sox lined up, and then Johnny Evers, who was the manager at that time, shaking hands with King George V. And there's the Duke of Wales, the Prince of Wales behind him, and the Duke of York behind him, later Edward VIII and George VI, and a few other dignitaries.

1579
01:23:35,082 --> 01:23:36,223
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite an interesting picture.

1580
01:23:36,243 --> 01:23:37,323
[SPEAKER_01]: I would like to have had that.

1581
01:23:37,363 --> 01:23:40,585
[SPEAKER_01]: And about eight or nine White Sox were well-known in that picture also.

1582
01:23:41,786 --> 01:23:45,708
[SPEAKER_01]: But I didn't bring my 35-millimeter camera with me, so I couldn't take shots of that.

1583
01:23:45,728 --> 01:23:46,969
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said he didn't have any.

1584
01:23:47,989 --> 01:23:49,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because he said he didn't have any.

1585
01:23:51,311 --> 01:23:54,092
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd gone to the Texas Hall of Fame or the, I don't know.

1586
01:23:54,973 --> 01:23:56,974
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a feeling he just discounted a lot of that.

1587
01:23:58,175 --> 01:24:02,457
[SPEAKER_01]: I would not even have seen those few pictures in the back of the kitchen had his sister not mentioned them.

1588
01:24:03,857 --> 01:24:05,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I was back there.

1589
01:24:05,057 --> 01:24:08,638
[SPEAKER_01]: I took his picture, holding up one of his own pictures.

1590
01:24:08,698 --> 01:24:11,098
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I asked his sister to take a picture of us.

1591
01:24:13,119 --> 01:24:14,619
[SPEAKER_01]: But she's got very poor eyesight.

1592
01:24:14,779 --> 01:24:29,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And she was holding the camera about a foot away from her head and said she couldn't see the little viewfinder through which she was supposed to gaze.

1593
01:24:30,242 --> 01:24:33,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, well, maybe you can get kind of a general view there.

1594
01:24:34,046 --> 01:24:37,967
[SPEAKER_01]: See, their camera's waving up and down and she wasn't looking very close.

1595
01:24:38,067 --> 01:24:41,487
[SPEAKER_01]: And Bib Fock rather abruptly says, can you see us?

1596
01:24:42,227 --> 01:24:44,468
[SPEAKER_01]: She says, no, I can't see you too.

1597
01:24:45,268 --> 01:24:46,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he said, don't click it then.

1598
01:24:46,628 --> 01:24:47,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And he marched out of the room.

1599
01:24:49,009 --> 01:24:50,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Thought he was very rude and abrupt.

1600
01:24:51,489 --> 01:24:54,930
[SPEAKER_01]: And I didn't see him again for about, I chatted with her for about five or 10 minutes.

1601
01:24:54,950 --> 01:24:57,230
[SPEAKER_01]: And I finally went out and he was watching the baseball game in the living room.

1602
01:24:59,370 --> 01:25:01,531
[SPEAKER_01]: He's abrasive a little bit.

1603
01:25:03,944 --> 01:25:06,565
[SPEAKER_01]: warm, the way I thought he would be.

1604
01:25:09,447 --> 01:25:11,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we watched the baseball game a little bit there.

1605
01:25:11,888 --> 01:25:15,730
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd actually autographed my ball before the interview began, so that was taken care of.

1606
01:25:17,111 --> 01:25:22,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And I took a couple pictures of him sitting down in the chair there we were, but I think the light was bad, and they may not come out.

1607
01:25:24,235 --> 01:25:32,499
[SPEAKER_01]: And so I departed, and he walked out to the car with me, and he told me about how bad it was on I-35 going back to camp when it was raining.

1608
01:25:34,662 --> 01:25:37,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was pleasant, congenial.

1609
01:25:37,523 --> 01:25:39,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe because I was leaving, I don't know.

1610
01:25:40,465 --> 01:25:50,569
[SPEAKER_01]: But I got there probably around 1.20 or so and left probably about 2.40.

1611
01:25:52,770 --> 01:25:58,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was a little over an hour interview and all the other things in there.

1612
01:25:58,992 --> 01:26:00,013
[SPEAKER_01]: As I say, I was not

1613
01:26:01,060 --> 01:26:04,001
[SPEAKER_01]: happy with the interview, but at least I saw Bibb Falk.

1614
01:26:04,122 --> 01:26:09,064
[SPEAKER_01]: I met him and talked with him and found out that you couldn't get a better interview than the one I got.

1615
01:26:11,145 --> 01:26:12,986
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it was pouring rain coming back.

1616
01:26:13,866 --> 01:26:20,430
[SPEAKER_01]: It got very heavy and I said, I'm sure, I thought to myself, I'm sure glad it isn't raining like this out at the KOA campground.

1617
01:26:26,923 --> 01:26:32,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I finally pulled into the campground, and I had trouble finding our tent.

1618
01:26:32,766 --> 01:26:34,788
[SPEAKER_01]: There was water all around.

1619
01:26:36,028 --> 01:26:39,150
[SPEAKER_01]: The tent was practically underwater, at least part of it was.

1620
01:26:40,211 --> 01:26:46,754
[SPEAKER_01]: I tooted the horn, wondering where Rita was, if she was swimming in the water or gone to some other place.

1621
01:26:47,795 --> 01:26:52,658
[SPEAKER_01]: And she appeared rather tattered and torn out from the entry.

1622
01:26:54,885 --> 01:26:57,827
[SPEAKER_01]: holding something that she was saving from the water, I guess.

1623
01:26:57,967 --> 01:26:58,828
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know what it was.

1624
01:26:58,988 --> 01:26:59,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Toothbrush, maybe.

1625
01:27:01,230 --> 01:27:04,352
[SPEAKER_01]: But right away, I realized that a crisis had occurred.

1626
01:27:05,874 --> 01:27:07,395
[SPEAKER_01]: And something had to be done quickly.

1627
01:27:09,256 --> 01:27:17,142
[SPEAKER_01]: So the tent itself was, the interior was dry for the most part.

1628
01:27:18,303 --> 01:27:21,806
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you walked on the floor, you were on about six inches of water.

1629
01:27:23,008 --> 01:27:25,750
[SPEAKER_01]: And the tent floor went up and down with each step.

1630
01:27:25,850 --> 01:27:34,595
[SPEAKER_01]: As you heard, the water underneath, the tent stakes at that edge of the tent were fully submerged, all four of them.

1631
01:27:35,516 --> 01:27:40,839
[SPEAKER_01]: And the water was seeping in through the seams at the bottom there and a little bit into the tent.

1632
01:27:41,420 --> 01:27:45,963
[SPEAKER_01]: She had everything up on the chairs and the cots we had in there.

1633
01:27:45,983 --> 01:27:49,585
[SPEAKER_01]: It was vaguely reminiscent of our sojourn in

1634
01:27:50,511 --> 01:27:58,595
[SPEAKER_01]: New York State in 1964, that bad rainy night at the World's Fair, remember, where the water was coming in all over the place.

1635
01:27:58,635 --> 01:28:06,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And we had about one square yard in the middle where four miserable people were huddled, two little squalling brats then.

1636
01:28:08,779 --> 01:28:10,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was raining all over the place.

1637
01:28:10,800 --> 01:28:12,301
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know how we ever lived through that night.

1638
01:28:13,981 --> 01:28:15,582
[SPEAKER_01]: We sure got out of there the next day, though.

1639
01:28:17,899 --> 01:28:25,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, well, this has nothing to do with the interview, but since I have a lot of tape to use up here, I might as well continue on to describe this miserable afternoon.

1640
01:28:27,744 --> 01:28:31,926
[SPEAKER_01]: We began taking things out of the tent to high ground.

1641
01:28:33,127 --> 01:28:38,309
[SPEAKER_01]: To the rear of our area, the ground inclined upward.

1642
01:28:38,950 --> 01:28:45,053
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was a rather nice grassy spot there we could move everything to, although it was not shady.

1643
01:28:45,530 --> 01:28:55,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And I just quickly sent Rita off to the telephone to get a motel for the night, because we were not going to stay in that place another night.

1644
01:28:59,319 --> 01:29:06,283
[SPEAKER_01]: But as we were removing all of the interior material to the exterior, the sun broke through.

1645
01:29:08,024 --> 01:29:10,165
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the sky became blue.

1646
01:29:11,526 --> 01:29:14,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And it became warm and beautiful and sunny.

1647
01:29:15,504 --> 01:29:16,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Beautiful afternoon.

1648
01:29:18,046 --> 01:29:26,192
[SPEAKER_01]: And we began to have second thoughts about going to that $30 motel up the road.

1649
01:29:27,573 --> 01:29:32,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So we had till 6 o'clock to declare ourselves.

1650
01:29:32,837 --> 01:29:36,120
[SPEAKER_01]: We did have a motel room saved till 6 o'clock.

1651
01:29:36,200 --> 01:29:41,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So we decided to wait and see.

1652
01:29:41,812 --> 01:29:46,435
[SPEAKER_01]: clear out the tent, for one thing, and take the tent down and dry it out if we could.

1653
01:29:47,776 --> 01:29:49,337
[SPEAKER_01]: So we did that.

1654
01:29:49,837 --> 01:29:50,618
[SPEAKER_01]: We got everything out.

1655
01:29:50,658 --> 01:29:51,518
[SPEAKER_01]: We pulled the tent.

1656
01:29:52,259 --> 01:30:01,605
[SPEAKER_00]: We managed to get the tent down without too much more water getting into it.

1657
01:30:03,866 --> 01:30:05,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Rita has something to say here.

1658
01:30:06,853 --> 01:30:07,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not really.

1659
01:30:07,913 --> 01:30:09,614
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that it was so tough.

1660
01:30:09,894 --> 01:30:11,935
[SPEAKER_00]: We took down the dry end first.

1661
01:30:12,975 --> 01:30:22,878
[SPEAKER_00]: And then while we both held up the end of the other, the wet end, we collapsed it and quickly pulled it over on dry ground.

1662
01:30:24,138 --> 01:30:26,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it was just half the tent that was under water.

1663
01:30:26,999 --> 01:30:29,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you for that description.

1664
01:30:31,641 --> 01:30:33,661
[SPEAKER_01]: OK, now we got the tent over on dry ground.

1665
01:30:34,421 --> 01:30:35,962
[SPEAKER_01]: And with the bottom side up.

1666
01:30:37,057 --> 01:30:39,779
[SPEAKER_01]: and the sun blazing down in beautiful warmth.

1667
01:30:41,300 --> 01:30:44,041
[SPEAKER_01]: The bottom of that tent was dry in about 10 minutes, probably.

1668
01:30:45,062 --> 01:30:47,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So then we flipped it over and dried the rest of it.

1669
01:30:47,483 --> 01:30:52,706
[SPEAKER_01]: And meanwhile, drying all of our drop cloths, which were soaked, our towels, which were soaked.

1670
01:30:53,146 --> 01:30:56,228
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, one towel will never be the same.

1671
01:30:56,428 --> 01:31:01,491
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure what color it is now, but it's not what it was originally.

1672
01:31:01,571 --> 01:31:06,234
[SPEAKER_01]: We dried out the tent case.

1673
01:31:07,335 --> 01:31:15,479
[SPEAKER_01]: bag, the tent pole bag, ourselves, and everything else.

1674
01:31:15,559 --> 01:31:17,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So we had enough room to dry these things.

1675
01:31:18,060 --> 01:31:20,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And it kept getting nicer and beautiful.

1676
01:31:20,401 --> 01:31:24,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Blue sky all over the place now towards 4 o'clock, 4.30.

1677
01:31:25,083 --> 01:31:29,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And we even had time to sit down and sip a beer and a Pepsi Cola or something.

1678
01:31:30,265 --> 01:31:31,866
[SPEAKER_01]: And life looked beautiful again.

1679
01:31:34,347 --> 01:31:34,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So

1680
01:31:36,707 --> 01:31:37,948
[SPEAKER_02]: We had to make a decision.

1681
01:31:38,348 --> 01:31:44,030
[SPEAKER_01]: We had till 6 o'clock and I finally made this bold decision that we would stay.

1682
01:31:44,070 --> 01:31:48,972
[SPEAKER_01]: We would re-set our tent in this dry area.

1683
01:31:49,332 --> 01:31:53,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Meanwhile, that area we evacuated was still under a foot of water practically.

1684
01:31:53,714 --> 01:32:02,597
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the lowest, I think it's the lowest plot of ground in Texas, or South Texas, wherever South Texas is.

1685
01:32:07,207 --> 01:32:09,989
[SPEAKER_01]: So we set the tent up, and it dried out nicely.

1686
01:32:10,049 --> 01:32:11,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And the interior was nice and dry.

1687
01:32:13,171 --> 01:32:14,672
[SPEAKER_01]: And they put everything back in there.

1688
01:32:14,692 --> 01:32:16,653
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was beautiful the rest of the evening.

1689
01:32:16,693 --> 01:32:20,556
[SPEAKER_01]: And we had a nice sleep there last night.

1690
01:32:21,616 --> 01:32:27,140
[SPEAKER_01]: And got up all chipper today to tear off to Austin and see the town.

1691
01:32:27,560 --> 01:32:30,182
[SPEAKER_01]: We got up this morning and had breakfast.

1692
01:32:31,457 --> 01:32:34,158
[SPEAKER_01]: packed everything, took the tent down, put it up on the roof.

1693
01:32:34,338 --> 01:32:35,999
[SPEAKER_01]: And we had the biggest roof we ever had.

1694
01:32:36,019 --> 01:32:36,939
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know what we put up there.

1695
01:32:36,959 --> 01:32:37,759
[SPEAKER_01]: We didn't do before.

1696
01:32:37,819 --> 01:32:41,981
[SPEAKER_01]: But I thought it was going up to the sky somewhere.

1697
01:32:43,141 --> 01:32:45,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody passed by and says, you've got a big load there, don't you?

1698
01:32:48,083 --> 01:32:53,244
[SPEAKER_01]: And so we got off about 9 o'clock or so, went into Austin, drove around.

1699
01:32:53,724 --> 01:32:56,705
[SPEAKER_01]: I got out to this ballpark Bibb Falk described, took a few pictures.

1700
01:32:56,725 --> 01:32:58,346
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a fantastic setup.

1701
01:32:58,993 --> 01:33:18,749
[SPEAKER_01]: And we drove back over to the university and got a picture of the football field, the LBJ library, the Texas Tower, and wandered around a little bit back through town, took a picture of the Capitol, and then down the road to 290 out to Fredericksburg, which is where we are now.

1702
01:33:19,109 --> 01:33:22,492
[SPEAKER_00]: You were going to talk about that other interview you had.

1703
01:33:22,672 --> 01:33:23,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that goes on the other tape.

1704
01:33:24,334 --> 01:33:27,236
[SPEAKER_01]: That goes on my other tape, but I just put that one in there.

1705
01:33:28,606 --> 01:33:30,115
[SPEAKER_01]: And we passed the Elgin.

